  "info": {
    "_postman_id": "b4e3f4a0-6b1a-4b2e-9d3a-neoaccountingz01",
    "name": "NeoAccounting \u2013 Invoices & Receipts API",
    "description": "Third-party REST API for NeoAccounting \u2014 Invoices and Receipts (Payments Received) only, including the Receipt apply/unapply actions.\n\n## Before you run this\n1. Set `base_url` (collection variable) to your NeoAccounting domain, e.g. `https://your-domain.com`.\n2. Set `api_key` (collection variable) to a key generated from Settings \u2192 Integrations \u2192 API Keys in the app. It's sent as `Authorization: Bearer {{api_key}}` on every request (set at the collection level).\n3. Set `customer_id` and `bank_account_id` to real ids from your organization (open a customer or bank account in the app \u2014 the id is in the URL), or grab them from a `GET /api/v1/customers` call if you have the fuller API collection.\n\n## How requests chain together\n\"Create Invoice\" and \"Create Receipt\" each save the id they get back into the `invoice_id` / `receipt_id` collection variables automatically (see each request's Tests tab), so every other request in this collection already points at the record you just created \u2014 run top to bottom inside a folder and it just works.\n\n## Auth\nEvery request needs `Authorization: Bearer <key>` or an `X-API-Key: <key>` header. This collection sets the Bearer header once at the collection level so every request inherits it.\n\n## Response validation\nEvery request in this collection has a **Tests** script that runs automatically after the response comes back \u2014 it checks the status code and confirms the JSON matches the shape documented in the API reference (e.g. `data.id` on create, `data.header`/`data.lines` on a get). Open the **Test Results** tab after running a request (or the Runner's summary when running the whole collection) to see pass/fail for each check. Several requests also carry saved **Examples** (visible in the sidebar under the request) showing a real success response and the documented error responses, so you can see the expected shape without calling the API at all.\n\n## Response shape\nSuccess: `{ \"data\": ... }`. Error: `{ \"error\": \"...\" }` with a non-2xx status (400 bad request/invalid state, 401 bad key, 404 not found, 500 server error).",
